# Optional recipe-source API keys
# --------------------------------
# The app works with NO keys (TheMealDB + a category fallback). Add keys to a
# real local `.env` file (Vite auto-loads `.env`, which is gitignored) to enable
# the larger / more region-aware recipe sources. Never commit a real key — only
# this `.env.example` template is tracked.

# --- Spoonacular (2nd source, larger) --------------------------------------
# Register for a free key at https://spoonacular.com
# VITE_SPOONACULAR_API_KEY=your-spoonacular-key

# --- Edamam (3rd source, best regional coverage) ---------------------------
# NOTE: Edamam's Recipe API is PAID only — there is no free tier (plans start
# around $9/mo, 10-day trial on some plans). Only add these if you intend to
# pay. If unset, the app silently skips Edamam and uses the free sources.
# VITE_EDAMAM_APP_ID=your-edamam-app-id
# VITE_EDAMAM_APP_KEY=your-edamam-app-key

# --- DeepSeek (optional AI recipe suggestions) -----------------------------
# PAID (cheap), no free tier. Get a key at https://platform.deepseek.com/api_keys
# When set, the app asks the model for a random, region-appropriate dish and
# adds it to the pool (clearly marked "suggested by AI"). Instructions are
# AI-written, not verified — the real verified recipes remain primary.
# VITE_DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=your-deepseek-key
# VITE_DEEPSEEK_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash

# --- Bing Images (optional, for AI dish photos) ------------------------------
# When set, AI recipe ideas get photos from the Bing Image Search API (more
# relevant than the default keyless Openverse pool). No search engine needed —
# a single Azure key searches the whole web. Free tier ≈ 1000 calls/month.
# 1) In the Azure Portal, create a resource: "Bing Search v7" (get Azure portal
#    https://portal.azure.com → Create a resource → search "Bing Search v7").
# 2) After creation, open it → Keys and Endpoint → copy one of the keys.
# VITE_BING_SUBSCRIPTION_KEY=your-bing-subscription-key

# --- Server storage & backups (read by server.mjs when deployed) ----------
# Your Saved Recipes are stored on the server (not just in each browser), so
# they survive browser/device changes. The live file lives in CQ_DATA_DIR and
# is auto-backed up to CQ_BACKUP_DIR. Both are OPTIONAL with defaults.
#
# CQ_DATA_DIR
#   Folder that holds the live saved-recipes file (favorites.json).
#   Default: data   (a folder inside the repo, gitignored)
#
# CQ_BACKUP_DIR
#   Folder where timestamped backups are written (favorites-<timestamp>.json).
#   A backup is made every time you save/remove a recipe, plus periodically.
#   Point this at a backed-up location (e.g. a OneDrive-synced or external
#   drive folder) so your Recipe Book is recoverable.
#   Default: backup  (a folder inside the repo, gitignored)
#
# CQ_BACKUP_INTERVAL_MS
#   How often (ms) to automatically back up even when nothing was saved, as a
#   safety net against losing history. 6h = 21600000.
#   Default: 21600000
#
# Paths may be absolute (C:\... or /...) or relative to the repo root.
# Examples:
#   CQ_DATA_DIR=C:\CulinaryData
#   CQ_BACKUP_DIR=D:\Backups\CulinaryQuest
#   CQ_BACKUP_INTERVAL_MS=3600000
#
# NOTE: these are used by the NODE server (server.mjs), not the Vite dev server.
# They take effect when you run `npm start` / deploy\start-server.bat.


